  "title": "Chords on the keyboard",
  "summary": "Triad shapes, inversions, and moving between chords with the least motion.",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p3.1",
      "title": "Triad shapes",
      "summary": "Major and minor triads under the hand.",
      "minutes": 8,
      "skillIds": [
        "p.blockChords",
        "m.triads"
      ],
      "reviewState": "draft",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A triad is a three-note chord built by skipping every other note of the scale: play one, skip one, play one, skip one, play one. With the notes this close together it sits under fingers 1, 3 and 5 of the right hand, or 5, 3 and 1 of the left."
        },
        {
          "type": "chords",
          "chords": [
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 0
            },
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "minor",
              "inversion": 0
            }
          ],
          "chordLabels": [
            "C major",
            "C minor"
          ],
          "text": "The middle note decides major or minor."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Press all three keys at exactly the same moment, with a relaxed drop of the arm. Then release and let the hand stay loose."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex16",
            "kind": "playChord",
            "prompt": "Play a C major chord.",
            "chord": {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 0
            },
            "requireInversion": false
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex17",
            "kind": "playChord",
            "prompt": "Play an A minor chord.",
            "chord": {
              "root": "A",
              "quality": "minor",
              "inversion": 0
            },
            "requireInversion": false
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "generated",
          "generator": {
            "kind": "chordPlay",
            "count": 6,
            "keys": [
              "C",
              "F",
              "G",
              "D",
              "A",
              "E"
            ],
            "qualities": [
              "major",
              "minor"
            ]
          },
          "text": "Now play each chord the app asks for. Any octave is fine."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3.2",
      "title": "Inversions",
      "summary": "The same three notes with a different one at the bottom.",
      "minutes": 10,
      "skillIds": [
        "p.inversions",
        "m.triads"
      ],
      "reviewState": "draft",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You can play the notes of a chord in any order. When the root is at the bottom the chord is in root position. Move the bottom note up an octave and you get the first inversion; do it again for the second inversion."
        },
        {
          "type": "chords",
          "chords": [
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 0
            },
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 1
            },
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 2
            }
          ],
          "chordLabels": [
            "root position",
            "first inversion",
            "second inversion"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Usual right-hand fingerings are 1 3 5 for root position, 1 2 5 for first inversion and 1 3 5 for second inversion. Let your hand choose what is comfortable; the app does not know which fingers you use."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex18",
            "kind": "playChord",
            "prompt": "Play C major in first inversion: E at the bottom.",
            "chord": {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 1
            },
            "requireInversion": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex19",
            "kind": "playChord",
            "prompt": "Play C major in second inversion: G at the bottom.",
            "chord": {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 2
            },
            "requireInversion": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex20",
            "kind": "playChord",
            "prompt": "Play F major in second inversion: C at the bottom.",
            "chord": {
              "root": "F",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 2
            },
            "requireInversion": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "generated",
          "generator": {
            "kind": "chordPlay",
            "count": 6,
            "keys": [
              "C",
              "F",
              "G"
            ],
            "qualities": [
              "major"
            ],
            "inversions": true
          },
          "text": "Play each chord with the named note at the bottom."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3.3",
      "title": "Changing chords smoothly",
      "summary": "Use inversions so the hand hardly moves.",
      "minutes": 10,
      "skillIds": [
        "p.inversions",
        "m.roman"
      ],
      "reviewState": "draft",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If you play C, F and G all in root position, the hand has to jump. Use inversions instead and the chords sit right next to each other: notes that two chords share stay where they are, and the other fingers move by a step."
        },
        {
          "type": "chords",
          "chords": [
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 0
            },
            {
              "root": "F",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 2
            },
            {
              "root": "G",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 1
            },
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 0
            }
          ],
          "chordLabels": [
            "C",
            "F (C at the bottom)",
            "G (B at the bottom)",
            "C"
          ],
          "text": "From C to F the bottom note C stays. From C to G the top note G stays."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex21",
            "kind": "playProgression",
            "prompt": "Play C, then F with C at the bottom, then G with B at the bottom, then C again.",
            "chords": [
              {
                "root": "C",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 0
              },
              {
                "root": "F",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 2
              },
              {
                "root": "G",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 1
              },
              {
                "root": "C",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 0
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              "C",
              "F/C",
              "G/B",
              "C"
            ],
            "explanation": "These are the three main chords of C major, played without moving the hand away."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before each change, look for the note the two chords share and keep that finger down."
        },
        {
          "type": "songLink",
          "songId": "when-the-saints",
          "text": "When the Saints uses exactly these three shapes in the left hand."
        },
        {
          "type": "songLink",
          "songId": "ode-to-joy",
          "text": "Ode to Joy moves between C and G with B at the bottom."
        }
      ]
    }
